 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "This review of autoinflammatory diseases summarises one previously published HA20 patient with individual-level detail: 'We previously described a 17-year-old Japanese boy with a TNFAIP3 mutation (Cys200Alafs*16)'.",
 "method_note": "Narrative review (Migita et al.); hand-extracted, no script transcription. In scope: the boy from the authors' earlier report (ref 33), plus his mother and sister, described as affected (oral and genital ulcers) but not stated to be tested; they get no variant rows. Labels: rule 4 ('ref (33) patient') since the review gives no label; the citation (33) follows the sentence about the mother and sister, and Figure 1 legend says it is modified from reference 33 and refers to 'our case'/'our patient'. The Figure 1 legend prints the cDNA as 'c597-598 T deletion' (no 'c.' period, malformed) and the protein as 'p.Cys200Alafs*16'; recorded as printed; the legend also says 'OUT domain' (sic, OTU). Zygosity not recorded: the only heterozygosity statement is about HA20 in general. Inheritance not reported: mother affected but no carrier statement. 'A20 haploinsufficiency' as disease label comes from the Figure 1 legend about the patient's mutation. The low-grade fever is not described as recurrent, so checklist 'recurrent fever' left not reported; epigastralgia is a symptom and does not establish GI ulcers or inflammation. Sister 'suspected of presenting with BD symptoms' recorded only as disease label, not as a feature. The patient's age (17-year-old) is recorded as age at last evaluation (age at description). Other patients in the review (the 18-year-old girl with intestinal BD/trisomy 8 MDS, Japanese patients with missense TNFAIP3 variants Ile310Thr/Gln709Arg in ref 32 without individual detail) are out of scope or lack individual-level facts. No contradictions found.",
